1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,909
In nineteen forty-five,

2
00:00:01,909 --> 00:00:05,147
most American families lived in cities,

3
00:00:05,147 --> 00:00:07,140
small towns or on farms.

4
00:00:07,320 --> 00:00:08,648
Fifteen years later,

5
00:00:08,648 --> 00:00:10,906
tens of millions of them had moved

6
00:00:10,906 --> 00:00:11,836
somewhere new.

7
00:00:12,016 --> 00:00:15,314
Somewhere that, just a few years before,

8
00:00:15,314 --> 00:00:17,292
had been a potato field,

9
00:00:17,292 --> 00:00:19,683
a cow pasture or a stretch of

10
00:00:19,683 --> 00:00:20,178
woods.

11
00:00:20,358 --> 00:00:22,343
They had moved to the suburbs.

12
00:00:22,693 --> 00:00:24,945
It was one of the biggest migrations

13
00:00:24,945 --> 00:00:26,197
in American history,

14
00:00:26,197 --> 00:00:28,637
and it happened in a single generation.

15
00:00:28,817 --> 00:00:30,306
By nineteen sixty,

16
00:00:30,306 --> 00:00:33,366
about one in three Americans lived in

17
00:00:33,366 --> 00:00:34,111
a suburb.

18
00:00:34,291 --> 00:00:37,289
Entire new towns appeared on the map,

19
00:00:37,289 --> 00:00:39,396
with names like Levittown,

20
00:00:39,396 --> 00:00:42,313
Park Forest, Lakewood and Daly City.

21
00:00:42,664 --> 00:00:44,416
So what was it really like to

22
00:00:44,416 --> 00:00:47,075
live in the suburbs in nineteen fifty-eight?

23
00:00:47,255 --> 00:00:49,387
And why did so many families leave

24
00:00:49,387 --> 00:00:50,390
the city behind?

25
00:00:50,570 --> 00:00:52,333
Let's go back and find out,

26
00:00:52,333 --> 00:00:54,814
from the cramped city apartment to the

27
00:00:54,814 --> 00:00:56,642
brand-new house with a yard.

28
00:00:56,992 --> 00:00:59,056
To understand why people left,

29
00:00:59,056 --> 00:01:01,466
you have to picture where they came

30
00:01:01,466 --> 00:01:01,810
from.

31
00:01:01,990 --> 00:01:02,957
After the war,

32
00:01:02,957 --> 00:01:04,892
American cities were packed.

33
00:01:05,072 --> 00:01:08,419
Families lived in small apartments in old

34
00:01:08,419 --> 00:01:12,663
brick buildings, sometimes three generations in four

35
00:01:12,663 --> 00:01:13,153
rooms.

36
00:01:13,333 --> 00:01:16,150
Children slept two or three to a

37
00:01:16,150 --> 00:01:16,502
bed.

38
00:01:16,682 --> 00:01:18,991
The bathroom might be down the hall,

39
00:01:18,991 --> 00:01:20,722
shared with other families.

40
00:01:21,073 --> 00:01:23,720
There was also a terrible housing shortage.

41
00:01:23,900 --> 00:01:26,325
During the Depression and the war,

42
00:01:26,325 --> 00:01:28,822
almost no new homes had been built.

43
00:01:29,002 --> 00:01:31,429
When millions of soldiers came home,

44
00:01:31,429 --> 00:01:33,855
there was simply nowhere for them to

45
00:01:33,855 --> 00:01:34,192
live.

46
00:01:34,372 --> 00:01:36,601
Young couples moved in with their parents.

47
00:01:36,781 --> 00:01:38,591
Some lived in garages,

48
00:01:38,591 --> 00:01:42,621
basements, old streetcars and even chicken coops.

49
00:01:42,971 --> 00:01:45,306
City life could also be noisy,

50
00:01:45,306 --> 00:01:46,395
dirty and hot.

51
00:01:46,575 --> 00:01:48,491
Coal smoke darkened the air.

52
00:01:48,671 --> 00:01:52,084
Factories were right next to apartment buildings.

53
00:01:52,264 --> 00:01:54,439
Children played in the street,

54
00:01:54,439 --> 00:01:55,527
dodging trucks.

55
00:01:55,707 --> 00:01:57,917
And as the city grew more crowded,

56
00:01:57,917 --> 00:02:00,843
many families dreamed of something better for

57
00:02:00,843 --> 00:02:01,558
their kids.

58
00:02:01,738 --> 00:02:02,481
A yard.

59
00:02:02,661 --> 00:02:03,311
A tree.

60
00:02:03,491 --> 00:02:04,374
Fresh air.

61
00:02:04,724 --> 00:02:06,233
And there was the baby boom.

62
00:02:06,413 --> 00:02:09,310
Young couples were having children faster than

63
00:02:09,310 --> 00:02:11,010
any generation before them.

64
00:02:11,190 --> 00:02:13,575
A family with three or four small

65
00:02:13,575 --> 00:02:16,177
kids simply didn't fit in a two-room

66
00:02:16,177 --> 00:02:18,635
apartment on the fourth floor of a

67
00:02:18,635 --> 00:02:19,213
walk-up.

68
00:02:19,393 --> 00:02:22,159
Mothers carried strollers up and down the

69
00:02:22,159 --> 00:02:23,305
stairs every day.

70
00:02:23,485 --> 00:02:25,575
A house with a yard wasn't a

71
00:02:25,575 --> 00:02:26,098
luxury.

72
00:02:26,278 --> 00:02:27,996
It felt like a necessity.

73
00:02:28,346 --> 00:02:30,871
Several things came together to make the

74
00:02:30,871 --> 00:02:31,945
suburbs possible.

75
00:02:32,125 --> 00:02:33,262
The first was money.

76
00:02:33,442 --> 00:02:33,976
The G.

77
00:02:34,156 --> 00:02:34,493
I.

78
00:02:34,673 --> 00:02:37,704
Bill offered veterans home loans with little

79
00:02:37,704 --> 00:02:38,806
or nothing down.

80
00:02:38,986 --> 00:02:43,068
The Federal Housing Administration insured long-term mortgages

81
00:02:43,068 --> 00:02:44,780
with low monthly payments.

82
00:02:44,960 --> 00:02:47,540
Suddenly, buying a house in the suburbs

83
00:02:47,540 --> 00:02:50,121
could cost less each month than renting

84
00:02:50,121 --> 00:02:51,775
an apartment in the city.

85
00:02:52,125 --> 00:02:53,564
The second was the car.

86
00:02:53,744 --> 00:02:55,460
In nineteen forty-five,

87
00:02:55,460 --> 00:02:59,115
about twenty-five million cars were registered in

88
00:02:59,115 --> 00:02:59,712
America.

89
00:02:59,892 --> 00:03:01,197
By nineteen sixty,

90
00:03:01,197 --> 00:03:03,444
it was more than sixty million.

91
00:03:03,624 --> 00:03:04,413
With a car,

92
00:03:04,413 --> 00:03:06,850
a man could live twenty miles from

93
00:03:06,850 --> 00:03:09,360
his job and drive in every morning.

94
00:03:09,540 --> 00:03:12,812
The suburbs were built for the automobile,

95
00:03:12,812 --> 00:03:16,006
with driveways, garages and wide streets.

96
00:03:16,357 --> 00:03:18,017
The third was the highway.

97
00:03:18,197 --> 00:03:19,761
In nineteen fifty-six,

98
00:03:19,761 --> 00:03:23,742
President Eisenhower signed the Federal-Aid Highway Act,

99
00:03:23,742 --> 00:03:26,869
which created the interstate highway system.

100
00:03:27,049 --> 00:03:29,987
Forty-one thousand miles of new highways would

101
00:03:29,987 --> 00:03:32,413
connect the cities to the countryside,

102
00:03:32,413 --> 00:03:34,712
and make the suburbs easier to reach

103
00:03:34,712 --> 00:03:35,351
than ever.

104
00:03:35,701 --> 00:03:37,375
And the fourth was a new way

105
00:03:37,375 --> 00:03:38,092
of building.

106
00:03:38,272 --> 00:03:41,464
A builder named William Levitt figured out

107
00:03:41,464 --> 00:03:44,124
how to build houses like Henry Ford

108
00:03:44,124 --> 00:03:44,960
built cars.

109
00:03:45,140 --> 00:03:47,645
His crews poured concrete slabs,

110
00:03:47,645 --> 00:03:50,386
and teams of workers moved down the

111
00:03:50,386 --> 00:03:52,500
street, each doing one job.

112
00:03:52,680 --> 00:03:53,479
At the peak,

113
00:03:53,479 --> 00:03:56,140
they finished about thirty houses a day.

114
00:03:56,490 --> 00:03:59,961
Builders across the country copied Levitt's methods.

115
00:04:00,141 --> 00:04:01,914
In Lakewood, California,

116
00:04:01,914 --> 00:04:05,312
crews built more than seventeen thousand homes

117
00:04:05,312 --> 00:04:06,863
in about three years.

118
00:04:07,043 --> 00:04:08,153
In Park Forest,

119
00:04:08,153 --> 00:04:11,038
Illinois, a whole planned town rose out

120
00:04:11,038 --> 00:04:13,405
of the prairie south of Chicago.

121
00:04:13,585 --> 00:04:14,852
All over America,

122
00:04:14,852 --> 00:04:17,385
farmland at the edge of every city

123
00:04:17,385 --> 00:04:20,887
turned into streets lined with brand-new houses

124
00:04:20,887 --> 00:04:22,154
almost overnight.

125
00:04:22,504 --> 00:04:25,023
The first Levittown went up on Long

126
00:04:25,023 --> 00:04:26,247
Island, New York,

127
00:04:26,247 --> 00:04:28,622
starting in nineteen forty-seven.

128
00:04:28,802 --> 00:04:31,855
It had more than seventeen thousand houses.

129
00:04:32,035 --> 00:04:34,404
A basic house sold for about seven

130
00:04:34,404 --> 00:04:36,494
thousand nine hundred dollars.

131
00:04:36,674 --> 00:04:39,206
Veterans could buy one with no money

132
00:04:39,206 --> 00:04:41,738
down and a monthly payment of around

133
00:04:41,738 --> 00:04:42,722
sixty dollars.

134
00:04:43,073 --> 00:04:45,655
Each house came with a living room,

135
00:04:45,655 --> 00:04:48,090
a kitchen with modern appliances,

136
00:04:48,090 --> 00:04:51,337
two bedrooms, one bathroom and an unfinished

137
00:04:51,337 --> 00:04:51,780
attic.

138
00:04:51,960 --> 00:04:54,752
Some models even came with a built-in

139
00:04:54,752 --> 00:04:55,884
television set.

140
00:04:56,064 --> 00:04:57,638
Every house had a tree in the

141
00:04:57,638 --> 00:04:58,235
front yard.

142
00:04:58,415 --> 00:05:00,539
Lines of people waited for days to

143
00:05:00,539 --> 00:05:01,039
buy one.

144
00:05:01,389 --> 00:05:03,107
Moving day was a thrill.

145
00:05:03,287 --> 00:05:06,257
Families arrived with a borrowed truck,

146
00:05:06,257 --> 00:05:08,922
a few pieces of furniture and boxes

147
00:05:08,922 --> 00:05:09,684
of dishes.

148
00:05:09,864 --> 00:05:11,640
The lawns were still mud.

149
00:05:11,820 --> 00:05:14,101
The trees were thin little sticks held

150
00:05:14,101 --> 00:05:15,001
up with stakes.

151
00:05:15,181 --> 00:05:17,248
There were no sidewalks yet,

152
00:05:17,248 --> 00:05:19,535
and sometimes no paved streets.

153
00:05:19,715 --> 00:05:21,623
But the children ran from room to

154
00:05:21,623 --> 00:05:24,051
room, and the parents could hardly believe

155
00:05:24,051 --> 00:05:25,265
it was really theirs.

156
00:05:25,615 --> 00:05:29,550
Critics called the suburbs boring and identical.

157
00:05:29,730 --> 00:05:32,488
A famous song later mocked the little

158
00:05:32,488 --> 00:05:34,426
boxes made of ticky-tacky,

159
00:05:34,426 --> 00:05:36,661
that all looked just the same.

160
00:05:36,841 --> 00:05:38,889
But the families who lived there didn't

161
00:05:38,889 --> 00:05:39,152
care.

162
00:05:39,332 --> 00:05:41,133
They had a home of their own,

163
00:05:41,133 --> 00:05:42,437
and for most of them,

164
00:05:42,437 --> 00:05:44,300
it was the first time in their

165
00:05:44,300 --> 00:05:44,672
lives.

166
00:05:45,022 --> 00:05:46,566
So what was a day in the

167
00:05:46,566 --> 00:05:48,947
suburbs like in nineteen fifty-eight?

168
00:05:49,127 --> 00:05:50,128
In the morning,

169
00:05:50,128 --> 00:05:51,865
fathers drove off to work,

170
00:05:51,865 --> 00:05:54,069
or walked to the train station to

171
00:05:54,069 --> 00:05:56,673
catch the commuter train into the city.

172
00:05:56,853 --> 00:05:59,013
Mothers stayed home with the children.

173
00:05:59,193 --> 00:06:00,818
The streets went quiet,

174
00:06:00,818 --> 00:06:02,444
except for the milkman,

175
00:06:02,444 --> 00:06:04,564
the mailman and the sound of a

176
00:06:04,564 --> 00:06:05,625
vacuum cleaner.

177
00:06:05,975 --> 00:06:08,111
And the streets were full of children.

178
00:06:08,291 --> 00:06:10,055
In nineteen fifty-eight,

179
00:06:10,055 --> 00:06:12,261
the baby boom was at its peak.

180
00:06:12,441 --> 00:06:13,465
In some suburbs,

181
00:06:13,465 --> 00:06:15,831
there were more children than adults.

182
00:06:16,011 --> 00:06:17,809
Kids played in huge groups,

183
00:06:17,809 --> 00:06:19,541
from one yard to the next.

184
00:06:19,721 --> 00:06:21,282
Kickball in the street,

185
00:06:21,282 --> 00:06:23,047
tag through the backyards,

186
00:06:23,047 --> 00:06:25,016
and bike rides to the edge of

187
00:06:25,016 --> 00:06:27,732
the development, where the fields began.

188
00:06:28,081 --> 00:06:30,174
The ranch house was the star of

189
00:06:30,174 --> 00:06:30,984
the suburbs.

190
00:06:31,164 --> 00:06:32,163
Long and low,

191
00:06:32,163 --> 00:06:33,468
all on one floor,

192
00:06:33,468 --> 00:06:36,464
with a picture window facing the street

193
00:06:36,464 --> 00:06:39,076
and sliding glass doors to a patio

194
00:06:39,076 --> 00:06:39,767
out back.

195
00:06:39,947 --> 00:06:42,623
Inside, there might be a family room,

196
00:06:42,623 --> 00:06:44,286
a new idea at the time,

197
00:06:44,286 --> 00:06:46,889
where kids could play and the family

198
00:06:46,889 --> 00:06:50,144
could watch television without messing up the

199
00:06:50,144 --> 00:06:51,011
living room.

200
00:06:51,361 --> 00:06:54,430
Mothers formed close friendships over coffee in

201
00:06:54,430 --> 00:06:55,866
each other's kitchens.

202
00:06:56,046 --> 00:06:57,679
They shared recipes,

203
00:06:57,679 --> 00:06:59,557
babysat for each other,

204
00:06:59,557 --> 00:07:00,946
and joined clubs.

205
00:07:01,126 --> 00:07:03,237
Bridge clubs, garden clubs,

206
00:07:03,237 --> 00:07:05,270
church groups and the PTA.

207
00:07:05,450 --> 00:07:07,763
Many women who moved to the suburbs

208
00:07:07,763 --> 00:07:10,473
say those friendships lasted for the rest

209
00:07:10,473 --> 00:07:11,464
of their lives.

210
00:07:11,815 --> 00:07:14,775
On weekends, fathers became homeowners.

211
00:07:14,955 --> 00:07:16,473
They mowed the lawn,

212
00:07:16,473 --> 00:07:17,764
painted the trim,

213
00:07:17,764 --> 00:07:20,725
built a patio and grilled hamburgers on

214
00:07:20,725 --> 00:07:22,699
the new backyard barbecue.

215
00:07:22,879 --> 00:07:25,607
Do-it-yourself became a national hobby.

216
00:07:25,787 --> 00:07:28,426
Hardware stores and lumber yards did a

217
00:07:28,426 --> 00:07:29,607
booming business.

218
00:07:29,787 --> 00:07:32,492
A man's Saturday belonged to his house.

219
00:07:32,841 --> 00:07:35,217
Summer in the suburbs had its own

220
00:07:35,217 --> 00:07:35,721
sounds.

221
00:07:35,901 --> 00:07:38,374
Lawnmowers on Saturday morning.

222
00:07:38,554 --> 00:07:41,247
Sprinklers ticking across the grass.

223
00:07:41,427 --> 00:07:43,540
The bells of the ice cream truck.

224
00:07:43,720 --> 00:07:45,647
Screen doors slamming.

225
00:07:45,827 --> 00:07:48,138
Radios playing ball games on the patio.

226
00:07:48,318 --> 00:07:49,188
And at dusk,

227
00:07:49,188 --> 00:07:52,089
a dozen mothers calling a dozen children

228
00:07:52,089 --> 00:07:53,250
home for dinner,

229
00:07:53,250 --> 00:07:54,773
all at the same time.

230
00:07:55,122 --> 00:07:57,761
The new suburbs had to build everything

231
00:07:57,761 --> 00:07:58,640
from scratch.

232
00:07:58,820 --> 00:08:00,984
There were no schools at first,

233
00:08:00,984 --> 00:08:03,567
so children rode buses to old schools

234
00:08:03,567 --> 00:08:04,335
miles away.

235
00:08:04,515 --> 00:08:06,592
Then new schools went up,

236
00:08:06,592 --> 00:08:09,417
sometimes two or three in a single

237
00:08:09,417 --> 00:08:09,832
year.

238
00:08:10,012 --> 00:08:13,468
Churches started in living rooms and garages,

239
00:08:13,468 --> 00:08:15,388
then built new buildings.

240
00:08:15,568 --> 00:08:18,737
Shopping centers rose in empty fields.

241
00:08:19,087 --> 00:08:20,701
People joined everything.

242
00:08:20,881 --> 00:08:22,207
The Little League,

243
00:08:22,207 --> 00:08:23,312
the Cub Scouts,

244
00:08:23,312 --> 00:08:24,491
the Girl Scouts,

245
00:08:24,491 --> 00:08:26,701
the volunteer fire department,

246
00:08:26,701 --> 00:08:29,426
the Rotary Club and the Women's Club.

247
00:08:29,606 --> 00:08:31,435
They held block parties,

248
00:08:31,435 --> 00:08:35,016
Fourth of July parades and Christmas decoration

249
00:08:35,016 --> 00:08:35,701
contests.

250
00:08:35,881 --> 00:08:38,799
They were building a community from nothing,

251
00:08:38,799 --> 00:08:40,722
and many felt a real sense of

252
00:08:40,722 --> 00:08:41,385
belonging.

253
00:08:41,734 --> 00:08:43,244
And they shopped differently.

254
00:08:43,424 --> 00:08:46,465
Instead of walking to the corner grocer,

255
00:08:46,465 --> 00:08:49,354
families drove to the new supermarket,

256
00:08:49,354 --> 00:08:52,015
with huge aisles and a parking lot.

257
00:08:52,195 --> 00:08:53,958
In nineteen fifty-six,

258
00:08:53,958 --> 00:08:56,603
Southdale Center opened in Edina,

259
00:08:56,603 --> 00:08:59,489
Minnesota, the first fully enclosed,

260
00:08:59,489 --> 00:09:03,016
climate-controlled shopping mall in America.

261
00:09:03,196 --> 00:09:04,869
It was the shape of things to

262
00:09:04,869 --> 00:09:05,158
come.

263
00:09:05,507 --> 00:09:07,019
And on summer evenings,

264
00:09:07,019 --> 00:09:09,451
families drove to the drive-in movie,

265
00:09:09,451 --> 00:09:12,015
the drive-in restaurant and the new ice

266
00:09:12,015 --> 00:09:14,052
cream stand out on the highway.

267
00:09:14,232 --> 00:09:16,563
The suburbs created a whole new way

268
00:09:16,563 --> 00:09:18,493
of life built around the car.

269
00:09:18,673 --> 00:09:21,662
Station wagons became the family car of

270
00:09:21,662 --> 00:09:24,114
choice, big enough for the kids,

271
00:09:24,114 --> 00:09:26,949
the dog, the groceries and the Little

272
00:09:26,949 --> 00:09:27,868
League team.

273
00:09:28,219 --> 00:09:29,922
But the suburbs were not open to

274
00:09:29,922 --> 00:09:30,401
everyone.

275
00:09:30,581 --> 00:09:33,522
Many developments, including Levittown,

276
00:09:33,522 --> 00:09:36,462
had rules that kept Black families from

277
00:09:36,462 --> 00:09:36,990
buying.

278
00:09:37,170 --> 00:09:40,122
Banks and the government refused to back

279
00:09:40,122 --> 00:09:43,811
loans in neighborhoods where Black families lived,

280
00:09:43,811 --> 00:09:45,877
a practice called redlining.

281
00:09:46,057 --> 00:09:48,775
Black veterans who had served their country

282
00:09:48,775 --> 00:09:50,481
often couldn't use their G.

283
00:09:50,661 --> 00:09:51,056
I.

284
00:09:51,236 --> 00:09:53,708
Bill benefits to buy a suburban home.

285
00:09:54,059 --> 00:09:56,523
As white families moved out,

286
00:09:56,523 --> 00:09:58,987
many cities lost population,

287
00:09:58,987 --> 00:10:01,187
businesses and tax money.

288
00:10:01,367 --> 00:10:02,772
Neighborhoods declined.

289
00:10:02,952 --> 00:10:04,740
Downtown stores struggled.

290
00:10:04,920 --> 00:10:08,502
That pattern, which historians call white flight,

291
00:10:08,502 --> 00:10:11,061
shaped American cities for decades.

292
00:10:11,241 --> 00:10:13,907
It wasn't until the Fair Housing Act

293
00:10:13,907 --> 00:10:17,906
of nineteen sixty-eight that discrimination in housing

294
00:10:17,906 --> 00:10:19,461
was finally outlawed.

295
00:10:19,812 --> 00:10:22,017
The suburbs had other problems too.

296
00:10:22,197 --> 00:10:23,885
For mothers without a car,

297
00:10:23,885 --> 00:10:26,027
the days could feel very long and

298
00:10:26,027 --> 00:10:26,481
lonely.

299
00:10:26,661 --> 00:10:28,264
Everything was far away.

300
00:10:28,444 --> 00:10:30,691
Some women felt cut off from the

301
00:10:30,691 --> 00:10:33,150
world, and in nineteen sixty-three,

302
00:10:33,150 --> 00:10:36,030
Betty Friedan wrote about that feeling in

303
00:10:36,030 --> 00:10:38,208
her book The Feminine Mystique.

304
00:10:38,558 --> 00:10:40,972
Fathers spent hours a day commuting.

305
00:10:41,152 --> 00:10:44,194
Traffic jams became a new American problem.

306
00:10:44,374 --> 00:10:47,005
And critics worried that the suburbs were

307
00:10:47,005 --> 00:10:48,866
making Americans too similar,

308
00:10:48,866 --> 00:10:51,048
too focused on keeping up with the

309
00:10:51,048 --> 00:10:53,807
Joneses, the neighbors who always seemed to

310
00:10:53,807 --> 00:10:55,603
have a newer car or a bigger

311
00:10:55,603 --> 00:10:56,309
television.

312
00:10:56,659 --> 00:10:58,649
There was also the cost of all

313
00:10:58,649 --> 00:10:59,643
that new stuff.

314
00:10:59,823 --> 00:11:01,470
To fill the new house,

315
00:11:01,470 --> 00:11:03,416
families bought furniture,

316
00:11:03,416 --> 00:11:06,486
appliances, a lawnmower and a second car,

317
00:11:06,486 --> 00:11:07,683
often on credit.

318
00:11:07,863 --> 00:11:11,219
Installment buying grew rapidly through the fifties.

319
00:11:11,399 --> 00:11:14,820
Many young couples found themselves stretched thin,

320
00:11:14,820 --> 00:11:18,106
working overtime and juggling monthly payments to

321
00:11:18,106 --> 00:11:18,643
keep up.

322
00:11:18,994 --> 00:11:21,429
And yet, for millions of families,

323
00:11:21,429 --> 00:11:23,649
the move to the suburbs worked.

324
00:11:23,829 --> 00:11:24,927
They had space,

325
00:11:24,927 --> 00:11:26,465
safety and fresh air.

326
00:11:26,645 --> 00:11:29,336
Their children had good schools and yards

327
00:11:29,336 --> 00:11:30,058
to play in.

328
00:11:30,238 --> 00:11:32,451
They built equity in a home that,

329
00:11:32,451 --> 00:11:35,200
decades later, would help pay for college

330
00:11:35,200 --> 00:11:36,205
and retirement.

331
00:11:36,556 --> 00:11:38,951
For many people who grew up there,

332
00:11:38,951 --> 00:11:41,769
the fifties suburb was a wonderful place

333
00:11:41,769 --> 00:11:42,755
to be a child.

334
00:11:42,935 --> 00:11:44,398
Packs of kids.

335
00:11:44,578 --> 00:11:46,145
Bikes everywhere.

336
00:11:46,325 --> 00:11:48,237
Woods and fields at the end of

337
00:11:48,237 --> 00:11:48,938
the street.

338
00:11:49,118 --> 00:11:50,418
Mothers at home.

339
00:11:50,598 --> 00:11:52,741
A sense that the whole neighborhood was

340
00:11:52,741 --> 00:11:53,895
watching out for you.

341
00:11:54,246 --> 00:11:57,432
Today, more than half of all Americans

342
00:11:57,432 --> 00:11:58,773
live in suburbs.

343
00:11:58,953 --> 00:12:00,960
The houses have gotten bigger,

344
00:12:00,960 --> 00:12:02,966
the yards have gotten fancier,

345
00:12:02,966 --> 00:12:05,976
and the trees planted in nineteen fifty-eight

346
00:12:05,976 --> 00:12:07,046
have grown tall.

347
00:12:07,226 --> 00:12:09,455
But the basic dream is still the

348
00:12:09,455 --> 00:12:09,803
same.

349
00:12:09,983 --> 00:12:11,311
A house of your own,

350
00:12:11,311 --> 00:12:12,905
a little patch of grass,

351
00:12:12,905 --> 00:12:14,964
and a safe street for the kids.

352
00:12:15,314 --> 00:12:16,985
If you grew up in a fifties

353
00:12:16,985 --> 00:12:18,905
suburb, you were part of one of

354
00:12:18,905 --> 00:12:21,258
the great changes in American history.

355
00:12:21,438 --> 00:12:23,502
Your parents took a big risk,

356
00:12:23,502 --> 00:12:25,352
moved somewhere brand new,

357
00:12:25,352 --> 00:12:27,487
and built a life from scratch.

358
00:12:27,667 --> 00:12:28,897
And they did it for you.

359
00:12:29,248 --> 00:12:31,461
And many of the original houses are

360
00:12:31,461 --> 00:12:32,220
still there.

361
00:12:32,400 --> 00:12:33,731
In Levittown today,

362
00:12:33,731 --> 00:12:36,043
you can still find a few original

363
00:12:36,043 --> 00:12:38,916
Cape Cods, though most have been expanded

364
00:12:38,916 --> 00:12:40,317
with second stories,

365
00:12:40,317 --> 00:12:42,419
new porches and big additions.

366
00:12:42,599 --> 00:12:44,936
Their owners love to show visitors the

367
00:12:44,936 --> 00:12:47,027
old photos, and point out the tree

368
00:12:47,027 --> 00:12:48,934
that was once a little stick in

369
00:12:48,934 --> 00:12:49,426
the mud.

370
00:12:49,776 --> 00:12:51,621
So tell us in the comments.

371
00:12:51,801 --> 00:12:54,200
Did your family move to the suburbs

372
00:12:54,200 --> 00:12:55,981
in the fifties or sixties?

373
00:12:56,161 --> 00:12:57,543
Where did you move from,

374
00:12:57,543 --> 00:12:59,040
and where did you move to?

375
00:12:59,220 --> 00:13:01,498
What do you remember about that first

376
00:13:01,498 --> 00:13:01,867
house?

377
00:13:02,047 --> 00:13:03,464
We read every comment.

378
00:13:03,644 --> 00:13:05,854
Next time, we're looking at what it

379
00:13:05,854 --> 00:13:07,306
was like to be a dad in

380
00:13:07,306 --> 00:13:08,253
nineteen sixty.

381
00:13:08,433 --> 00:13:10,237
Thank you for spending this time with

382
00:13:10,237 --> 00:13:10,383
me.
